1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,594
<font color="#000000">From Peking University and Google,</font>

2
00:00:02,674 --> 00:00:06,815
<font color="#000000">CITransNet is a context-integrated transformer for
auction design.</font>

3
00:00:06,895 --> 00:00:10,843
<font color="#000000">It maximizes seller revenue while staying
incentive-compatible,</font>

4
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:12,825
<font color="#000000">discovers asymmetric solutions,</font>

5
00:00:12,905 --> 00:00:16,790
<font color="#000000">and generalizes to auction sizes it never saw
during training.</font>

6
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:22,656
<font color="#000000">A classic problem in auction theory is designing a
mechanism that maximizes the seller's</font>

7
00:00:22,736 --> 00:00:25,375
<font color="#000000">revenue while staying incentive-compatible.</font>

8
00:00:25,455 --> 00:00:30,562
<font color="#000000">Myerson solved the single-item case in 1981, but
the multi-item case remains open,</font>

9
00:00:30,642 --> 00:00:34,230
<font color="#000000">and prior deep methods assume fixed or symmetric auctions.</font>

10
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:36,162
<font color="#000000">Real auctions are richer.</font>

11
00:00:36,242 --> 00:00:37,860
<font color="#000000">In e-commerce advertising,</font>

12
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:41,842
<font color="#000000">many bidders compete for many ad slots described by
features,</font>

13
00:00:41,922 --> 00:00:45,433
<font color="#000000">and every round has a different number of participants.</font>

14
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:50,590
<font color="#000000">We need an architecture that absorbs context and
handles varying auction sizes.</font>

15
00:00:50,970 --> 00:00:53,106
<font color="#000000">The paper makes two contributions.</font>

16
00:00:53,186 --> 00:00:59,492
<font color="#000000">It extends the RegretNet framework to the
contextual setting and proves a sample-complexity bound.</font>

17
00:00:59,572 --> 00:01:04,510
<font color="#000000">And it introduces CITransNet,
permutation-equivariant over bids and contexts,</font>

18
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:06,921
<font color="#000000">not restricted to symmetric auctions,</font>

19
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:10,310
<font color="#000000">with a parameter count independent of auction scale.</font>

20
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:14,193
<font color="#000000">CITransNet takes three inputs: the bidding profile,
bidder-contexts,</font>

21
00:01:14,273 --> 00:01:17,776
<font color="#000000">and item-contexts.</font>

22
00:01:17,856 --> 00:01:21,359
<font color="#000000">It embeds contexts with bids into a representation
per bidder-item pair,</font>

23
00:01:21,439 --> 00:01:24,941
<font color="#000000">then passes it through transformer interaction
layers that mix a global average with row and column transformers,</font>

24
00:01:25,021 --> 00:01:28,524
<font color="#000000">staying permutation-equivariant.</font>

25
00:01:28,604 --> 00:01:32,107
<font color="#000000">A final layer outputs allocations and payments,</font>

26
00:01:32,187 --> 00:01:35,690
<font color="#000000">with zero-regret enforced during training.</font>

27
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:38,736
<font color="#000000">They evaluate on nine synthetic settings.</font>

28
00:01:38,816 --> 00:01:41,214
<font color="#000000">Three single-item cases, A through C,</font>

29
00:01:41,294 --> 00:01:44,898
<font color="#000000">have known Myerson-optimal solutions as a sanity check.</font>

30
00:01:44,978 --> 00:01:50,591
<font color="#000000">The other six, D through I, are multi-item auctions
up to five bidders and ten items,</font>

31
00:01:50,671 --> 00:01:53,940
<font color="#000000">the largest size in the auction-design literature.</font>

32
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,164
<font color="#000000">Results come in two parts.</font>

33
00:01:56,244 --> 00:02:01,938
<font color="#000000">On single-item settings, CITransNet nearly recovers
Myerson's optimal revenue,</font>

34
00:02:02,018 --> 00:02:05,935
<font color="#000000">0.593 against 0.594, with regret below one thousandth,</font>

35
00:02:06,015 --> 00:02:08,970
<font color="#000000">while context-blind baselines fall short.</font>

36
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:11,486
<font color="#000000">On the harder multi-item settings,</font>

37
00:02:11,566 --> 00:02:16,371
<font color="#000000">it beats the Item-wise Myerson baseline in all six
configurations,</font>

38
00:02:16,451 --> 00:02:18,740
<font color="#000000">with gains reaching ten percent.</font>

39
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:20,618
<font color="#000000">To isolate why it works,</font>

40
00:02:20,698 --> 00:02:28,244
<font color="#000000">the authors replace the transformer layers with
RegretNet's fully-connected and EquivariantNet's equivariant layers,</font>

41
00:02:28,324 --> 00:02:30,874
<font color="#000000">giving CIRegretNet and CIEquivariantNet.</font>

42
00:02:30,954 --> 00:02:34,622
<font color="#000000">Both still get context, yet earn less revenue everywhere,</font>

43
00:02:34,702 --> 00:02:37,449
<font color="#000000">pinning the gain on the transformer layers.</font>

44
00:02:37,529 --> 00:02:40,210
<font color="#000000">Removing context hurts even easy settings.</font>

45
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:43,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

46
00:02:43,413 --> 00:02:50,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the single-item benchmark, CITransNet reaches
0.593 versus the optimal 0.594.</font>

47
00:02:50,256 --> 00:02:57,532
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the three-bidder ten-item setting it earns 6.872
against Item-wise Myerson's 6.509,</font>

48
00:02:57,612 --> 00:03:01,981
<font color="#FFFFFF">and one continuous-context margin nears ten percent.</font>

49
00:03:02,061 --> 00:03:05,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">It wins all six multi-item settings.</font>

50
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:10,531
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: context as a first-class input
unlocks better learned auctions.</font>

51
00:03:10,611 --> 00:03:15,702
<font color="#000000">CITransNet is near-optimal, asymmetric when needed,
and incentive-compatible.</font>

52
00:03:15,782 --> 00:03:22,350
<font color="#000000">Its size-independent parameter count lets one
trained model transfer to auction sizes it never saw.</font>
